          <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-serif font-bold text-navy mb-6 leading-snug" data-i18n="qa.q4.question">
            What powers does a county commissioner actually have?
          </h2>

          <div class="prose prose-lg text-near-black/85 font-sans leading-relaxed space-y-4">
            <p data-i18n="qa.q4.p1">
              A commissioner has a vote on the five-member Commissioners Court. That means helping set the county budget and tax rate, approving contracts, and making countywide decisions.
            </p>
            <p data-i18n="qa.q4.p2">
              Commissioners also supervise road operations in their precincts, making sure crews have what they need, setting maintenance priorities, dealing with drainage problems, and seeing that equipment and materials are put to good use.
            </p>
            <p data-i18n="qa.q4.p3">
              No one commissioner gets to dictate what the whole court does. You have to do your homework, make your case, and earn the other votes. And when a job falls under your supervision, people ought to be able to hold you accountable for getting it done.
            </p>
            <p data-i18n="qa.q4.p4">
              Each commissioner is a liaison for different departments. I am the Court's liaison for Economic Development and Tourism, as well as the liaison for the Animal Shelter.
            </p>
            <div class="p-6 bg-cream rounded-xl border border-warm-gray my-4">
              <h4 class="font-serif font-bold text-lg text-navy mb-2">Case Study in Action: The DarPro Resolution</h4>
              <p class="text-sm font-sans text-near-black/85 leading-relaxed" data-i18n="qa.q4.p5">
                An example of how I used these powers for the good of our community, is what I did to help with the DarPro issue. We started by monitoring for months with our new Bastrop County Health Department with the assistance of Texas A&M. They were able to substantiate the gases that were being emitted into the community. We had a graduate student that would travel from College Station with short notice to come do the testing of the air when the odor was bad. It took that to begin to build the patterns of noncompliance. I had many meetings after that with the DarPro team. They brought in a complete new management team. They agreed there was a horrendous problem and committed to making all improvements necessary to correct the situation. The initial commitment was 34 million dollars worth of improvements. I believe that has grown to approximately 50 million.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
